<Layout>
  <body>
  
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center;justify-content: center;">
    

    <div class="hero">
      <h1>Networks of Influence<br>in Environmental Lobbying</h1>
      
      <div class="content">
      <p>
        <b> Hook: SEE IDEAS IN COMMENTS. </b>
        How can we measure lobbyists' success at effecting change? Straight away, we might imagine a lobbyist's influence as proportional to money spent on advocacy - with that money buying votes or airtime for informational signaling.
        <br /> <br />
        But effective coalition is <i>social</i>. Well-connected networks of actors mean that political intelligence travels to where it needs to be and those with allied interests can strategically coordinate legislative labor. It's been suggested<sup>1</sup> that the most valuable work lobbyists do is to facilitate this: rather than changing legislators' minds, they coordinate between natural political allies to achieve goals that are desirable to everyone.
        <br> <br>
        Using <a href="https://www.opensecrets.org">Open Secrets</a> data on environmental lobbying, we built networks associating and grouping actors by shared lobbying activity, across 20 years and 12 federal agencies. <a href="INSERT_ANCHOR">Scroll</a> to follow our journey through the data, or take our explorer app for a spin!
        <br> <br>
        <em style="font-size: .7em;;">
        <sup>1</sup> Lobbying as Legislative Subsidy - Hall, Deardorff, 2006 
        <!-- <br> 
        idea: compare plain-spending and logged-spending nwks to show that in latter, interesting patterns of influence emerge that aren't completely dominated by businesses. -->
        </em>
      </p>
      <NwkView/>
      </div>
